No mention of Cambodian rice in Europe
The European Union (EU) Chamber of Commerce has never said that rice exported from Cambodia to Europe is mixed with Vietnamese rice.
The claim comes amid fears that Cambodia could lose access to one of its largest markets.
Cambodia's rice exports in the first 11 months of this year totaled more than 332,000 tonnes, almost double the same period last year of 171,000 tonnes last week. Oryza's industry publication quotes the European Chamber of Commerce's president as saying that Cambodia faces the loss of all programs except the EBA arms with the EU.
Unlike Vietnam, Cambodia benefits from the EBA agreement with the EU, which provides developing countries with quota-free and duty-free imports to Europe.
Poland, France, the Netherlands, Spain, the United Kingdom and Germany imported more than 47 percent of the total.
Mey Kalyan, a senior adviser to the Supreme National Economic Council, called on the Cambodian government to investigate the matter out of concern that the country's change in EBA status could have consequences.
Our pride as a growing exporter, our confidence in doing business, our brand as a global rice exporter, and we are unlikely to reach the 1 million tonne export target this year. 2015.
The Cambodian Coalition of Rice Producers and Exporters (ARPEC) said in a statement yesterday that all Cambodian rice exporters had noted the concerns that they would be able to sign a code of conduct with the Ministry of Commerce. Self-export.
In response to indications that some Cambodian rice could be mixed during passing through a Vietnamese rice mill, Van said that overseas processing did not mean that the two types of raw rice were mixed. He added that the short-term supply of Cambodian rice mills had prompted Cambodian farmers to send their rice to Vietnam for milling.
But rice cultivation, harvesting, milling and exporting in Cambodia are 100% our products.
