Cambodia Post Bank shows momentum after three months
With the remarkable growth momentum of the past, the Post Bank of Cambodia, which has only been operating for three months, has shown the momentum of competition in the financial sector in Cambodia.
The new bank is partnering with the Cambodia Post to become the largest banking network in Cambodia.
Touch Chao Chek, CEO of Post Bank of Cambodia, said:
In the next three years, we will be able to start collaborating on products and services.
We focus on lending for the establishment and expansion of businesses, businesses and small and medium enterprises.
To date, Cambodia Post Bank has 11 branches nationwide, attracting more than 2,000 customers.
The bank received about $ 10 million in deposits and more than $ 20 million in loans.
Aiming at the mass market customers, Cambodia Post Bank is seen to be providing similar services to microfinance institutions, creating competition for existing banks and financial institutions.
Sim Senacheat, CEO and chief executive officer of PRASAC, told the Post yesterday that new operators in the market meant greater competition in the sector.
For us, this new bank could be a major competitor.
However, we are not too worried because the Cambodian market is still big and PRASAC has built a lot of network in its 18 years of operation.
ACLEDA Bank President and CEO In Channy believes that having more financial institutions in a competitive market is a good thing.
Data from the National Bank shows that so far Cambodia has 34 commercial banks, seven specialized banks and 37 financial institutions.
Officials claim progress after reforms
Phnom Penh: Officials of the General Department of Customs confirmed yesterday that revenue from the collection of customs duties has increased since November, after the government set a 60-day deadline for the General Department of Customs to improve work efficiency as the deadline approaches. The end of December.
On October 25, Mr. Hun Sen criticized the corruption of customs officials and gave the General Department of Customs two months to correct its work.
Bou Bunnara, head of the public relations unit at the General Department of Customs and Excise, told the Post yesterday that the General Department of Customs had planned three short-term action plans to be implemented from November, including: Strengthen the work of collecting revenue from customs and excise, suppress the import of tax evaded goods and strengthen the governance of the General Department of Customs and Excise.
Kang Chandararot, president of the Institute for Development Studies in Cambodia, said this was a positive sign for long-term economic development.
In the past, Cambodia still relied on foreign aid and debt, and the repayment date was approaching, which had to find a source of income to support its economic base.
