A new finding shows that Cambodia is the least attractive destination for investment among ASEAN countries.
A 2013 ASEAN Competitiveness Survey released last week ranked Cambodia one point below Brunei in terms of investor attractiveness.
The survey found that only 21 per cent of the 502 respondents to the survey, all of them from the ASEAN business community, found that Cambodia was a country worthy of investment in the future.
Senior government officials have again called on local enterprises to improve quality and increase productivity to compete with the flow of foreign products as ASEAN economic integration approaches in 2015.
The call was made at a national workshop on modern food safety management systems last week.
The advent of the ASEAN Economic Community in 2015 means that goods within ASEAN will flow across borders duty-free.
Industry Minister Cham Prasidh saw it as an opportunity, but questioned whether Cambodian goods were ready for export at a time when domestic production was still limited in both quality and standards.
He expressed optimism that within two years, Cambodian goods could be strengthened to compete if local enterprises were willing to improve their standards and production.
Srey Chanthy, president of the Cambodian Economic Association, said that while other major economies in the region, such as Singapore, Malaysia and Thailand, were preparing for full AEC integration, Cambodia's arrangements were gaining less credibility. Little.
The Cambodian government yesterday signed a $ 26 million loan agreement with the French development agency AFD for irrigation rehabilitation projects in three provinces.
Minister of Economy and Finance Aun Porn Moniroth said at the signing ceremony that the loan from the agreement was part of an irrigation and agricultural development project that would rehabilitate 11 irrigated areas. The 11,000-hectare site is located in Kampong Thom, Siem Reap and Banteay Meanchey provinces, and 30 more canals will be rehabilitated in Kandal province, covering an area of 5,000 hectares.
Mr. Aun Porn Moniroth said:
This is the first concessional loan provided by the French development agency AFD to the Royal Government of Cambodia after it provided about $ 5.2 million in grants in March to the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology to strengthen its capacity. Manage water resources.
CEDAC project consultant Khim Sophanna said the challenge for Cambodian farmers was to rely on rainfall as a source of irrigation.
He said that additional irrigation systems in Cambodia, such as reservoirs, dams, canals, ponds, etc., in addition to rain, but not enough to irrigate the total cultivated area.
Data from CEDAC shows that Cambodia's supplementary irrigation system has a total of 2,000 canals and canals, but only 10 percent of irrigation can be used.
Regarding the project to rehabilitate the irrigation system, Mr. Khim Sophanna said:
In the past, we have seen a lot of construction, but the challenge is to maintain it for long-term use.
It is important that the loan is used in a targeted and efficient manner, because the construction costs a lot of money, if the construction is not of good quality, it will be damaged and it is time to repay them.
Cambodia has a total agricultural area of about 3 million hectares, with irrigation covering a total of 1.4 million hectares, accounting for 63% of the total agricultural area.
